  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nLow coverage light rain/sprinkle potential will continue through the \nremainder of the weekend but most areas will remain mainly dry.\n\nAn area of light rain/sprinkles has slowly migrated east-northeast \nacross northeast Indiana into northwest Ohio this afternoon and \nthese light rain showers appear to be tied to a zone of better \n290-300K isentropic forcing. A weak easterly flow regime has \nkept subcloud layer dry with most areas only experiencing a \ntrace to few hundredths of an inch. An exception to this was \nearly this morning across western Indiana where bands of better \namounts were realized.\n\nInto this late afternoon/early evening, guidance is in general \nagreement that the initial stronger isentropic lift should weaken as \nzone of favored advective forcing lifts across southern Lower \nMichigan. As we head toward overnight into Sunday AM, some \nindications that a second push of stronger isentropic forcing \nmay tend to lift south to north across east central Illinois \ninto west central Indiana as a more defined vort max approaches \nthe Mid MS River Valley. Despite the approach of this wave, low \nlevel moisture transport still looks limited locally and more \nfavorable back across the Mid MS Rvr Valley. This argues for \njust maintaining 20% mention of measurable precip mainly for \nwest/southwest locations. Sunday should feature a better chance \nof slightly better mixing that could support highs reaching \nnear 70 across the far south/southeast.\n\nWill still maintain dry conditions for Monday as the \naforementioned short wave trough dampens in a background \nsynoptic flow that continues to prefer longwave ridge \nmaintenance across central CONUS. Some better low level moisture\ntransport will begin to develop later in the day Monday as low \nlevel profiles begin to veer slightly, although still more \nimpressive deeper transport profiles are expected to remain \nanchored back to the west, and this better moisture transport \nwill only begin once mid/upper level forcing begins to wane.\n\nAttention for Tue/Wed will be on more amplified and larger scale \nupper trough expected to track across central CONUS. Despite high \namplitude, upper level forcing in terms of inducing low level height \nfalls looks rather modest, with much of low level negative height \ntendencies will likely be derived from a broad pocket of highly\nanomalous warm low level air. The upstream system also still\nlooks to occlude fairly quickly. Better moisture pooling should\nbe in relatively narrow frontal zone, but a period of likely \nPoPs still appears in order late Tuesday into Wednesday. \nInstability in occluded sector will be rather meager, although \ncannot completely rule out a rumble of thunder. A period of \nwindy conditions may develop on Tuesday with best chance of \ndeeper mixing during the afternoon hours. Tuesday should feature\nthe warmest day of the period with highs back into the low-mid \n70s (could approach records for the date).\n\nNo notable intrusion of significantly colder air will accompany\nfrontal passage midweek, with above normal temperatures \nexpected to continue through the end of next work week. Some low\nrain chances were maintained in the long term Day 4-7 period \nwith at least the possibility of southern stream disturbance \ndeveloping out of west coast split flow meandering across Ohio \nRiver Valley late in the work week.",
